Patch 81 -- skip the active-program selection that selects what is already
selected.

SCOPE: dlls/wined3d/glsl_shader.c. One field, one census, one helper, two call
sites, one line of A/B wiring. Ships OFF: this is a census first.

WHY THIS CALL AND NOT ANOTHER

Every other GL candidate in this project had to estimate what a call costs. This
one does not. P75A removed ~1380 of ~1572 glActiveShaderProgram calls per combat
frame and measured -14.80 ms/frame, which prices THIS call on THIS driver through
THIS emulator at

    14.80 ms / 1380 ~= 10.7 us

About 192 survive per frame, because an upload genuinely follows them. So the
ceiling is 192 x 10.7 us ~= 2.05 ms/frame and a 50% redundancy rate is worth
~1.0 ms. Not a fps headline; a real millisecond on a 70 ms frame.

WHY IT IS NOT ANOTHER FAILED SHADOW

Two GL shadow candidates were closed here for caching values under a wrong key.
This caches no value. glActiveShaderProgram(pipeline, program) chooses which
program subsequent glUniform* calls write into, and it is state OF THE PIPELINE
OBJECT -- GL exposes it as GL_ACTIVE_PROGRAM of that pipeline. Setting the same
program again is a semantic no-op with no payload.

So the key has to be the pipeline, and it is: the shadow is a field in
glsl_shader_prog_link, next to pipeline_id. That is a 1:1 key, checked rather than
assumed -- glGenProgramPipelines() is called per entry at glsl_shader.c:12013 and
12144 and the pipeline is deleted with the entry at 8355. The STAGE PROGRAMS are
shared between entries, which is exactly why the light cache is keyed on
vs_program_id, but the pipeline is not shared and it is the pipeline that owns the
state. A file-scope "last program" would be the old mistake in a new place.

calloc'd with the entry, and 0 is truthful rather than merely convenient: a fresh
pipeline's GL_ACTIVE_PROGRAM really is 0.

THE INVARIANT IS MEASURED, NOT ARGUED

MGS2_GL_APS_VERIFY=1 reads GL_ACTIVE_PROGRAM back with glGetProgramPipelineiv on
the first selection and every 4096th, and compares. A mismatch is counted, logged,
and disables the skip for the rest of the run -- so the run stays correct and the
counter says it is void. Not production: glGet* can be expensive. This exists so
the first correctness run reports mismatch=0 instead of resting on the source
audit above.

THE TRAP THAT WOULD HAVE VOIDED THE MEASUREMENT

Box86's island links a SECOND native ARM copy of glsl_shader.c, so there are two
mgs2_aps_stats records in the process. mgs2_texmatrix_ab_present() -- which writes
the arm -- runs on the PE side only, because the present path is not a routed
island entry. Under an unpinned ABBA the island's copy therefore sits at its
static enabled=0 for the whole run while the PE copy alternates: if
context_apply_draw_state reaches shader_glsl_load_constants natively, that half of
the work never sees arm B and the ABBA can only UNDER-report.

Two things follow, and both are in the patch. The helper consults MGS2_GL_APS
through getenv() rather than reading the struct the presenter writes, because
getenv works in both copies and a pinned arm is the only one both can follow. And
harness/gl_census_delta.py prints the APS1 records SEPARATELY instead of summing
them, so run 1 answers which copy does the work before anyone chooses between the
ABBA and two pinned runs. P75A had the same asymmetry and got away with it because
its production value was the static initialiser.

WHAT THE COUNTERS MEAN

  attempted   selections that reached P81, i.e. P75A did not already avoid them.
              P75A's avoided_vs/avoided_ps are the rest.
  redundant   ...and the program was already this pipeline's active program.
              Counted whether or not the skip is armed, so ONE census run gives
              the redundancy rate with behaviour identical to FINALPLAY14.
  skipped     ...and the skip was armed, so the call was not made.
  executed    the call was made. The shadow is updated on BOTH arms -- an arm
              starting from a stale shadow would measure the warm-up instead of
              the optimisation, the same reason the light cache updates its
              entries regardless of its own enable.

A GOOD RUN 1

  attempted 50000  redundant 35000 (70%)  skipped 0  executed 50000
  mismatch 0

Then arm it. If redundancy is near zero the candidate is closed on a census that
cost nothing, which is the point of shipping it off.

NEXT, AND DELIBERATELY NOT NOW

GL ES 3.1 has glProgramUniform*, which takes the program explicitly and needs no
selection at all -- that would remove all ~192 rather than the redundant share.
It also touches every uniform site in the file. Worth doing only if P81 shows the
redundancy is small, which is a thing run 1 answers for the price of a census.

